>11836525</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vokes</surname><given-names>EE</given-names></name><name><surname>Weichselbaum</surname><given-names>RR</given-names></name><name><surname>Lippman</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><name><surname>Hong</surname><given-names>WK</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Head and neck cancer</article-title><source>N Engl J Med</source><year>1993</year><volume>328</volume><fpage>184</fpage><lpage>194</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'N Engl J Med||328|184||bib16|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8417385</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Watkinson</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Gaze</surname><given-names>MN</given-names></name><name><surname>Wilson</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Tumours of the larynx</article-title><source>Stell &#x00026; Maran's Head and Neck Surgery</source><year>2000</year><publisher-name>Oxford: Butterworth Heinemann</publisher-name><fpage>233</fpage><lpage>275</lpage>In: Stell PM, Maran AGD, Watkinson JC (eds)</citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weiss</surname><given-names>SW</given-names></name><name><surname>Goldblum</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title/><source>Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumours</source><year>2001</year><publisher-name>St Louis: Mosby</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Williams</surname><given-names>GH</given-names></name><name><surname>Romanowski</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Morris</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Madine</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Mills</surname><given-names>AD</given-names></name><name><surname>Stoeber</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Marr</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Laskey</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Coleman</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Improved cervical smear assessment using antibodies against proteins that regulate DNA replication</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><year>1998</year><volume>95</volume><fpage>14932</fpage><lpage>14937</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Proc Natl Acad Sci USA||95|14932||bib19|'--></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Distribution of Mcm-2 and Ki67 in oral lesions. Immunohistochemical staining illustrating the distribution of Mcm-2 (left) and Ki67 (right) in biopsies of oral mucosa showing benign keratosis and mild, moderate and severe dysplasia. All images are at &#x000d7; 200 magnification.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="jpeg" xlink:href="94-6603066f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Frequency of Mcm-2 and Ki67 expression in oral lesions. Box plots showing labelling indices for Mcm-2 and Ki67 in different compartments of the oral epithelium in benign keratosis and in mild, moderate and severe dysplasia. Bars&#x0003d;median; boxes&#x0003d;IQR; whiskers&#x0003d;range of data or 1.5 IQRs from the end of the box; circles&#x0003d;outliers; stars&#x0003d;extreme outliers.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="jpeg" xlink:href="94-6603066f2"/></fig><fig id="fig3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Comparison of Mcm-2 and Ki67 labelling indices in the superficial epithelial third of oral lesions. There is a positive correlation between Mcm-2 and Ki67 LI values for the superficial third of the epithelium in benign keratosis (<italic>n</italic>&#x0003d;9), and in mild (<italic>n</italic>&#x0003d;17), moderate (<italic>n</italic>&#x0003d;16) and severe (<italic>n</italic>&#x0003d;17) dysplasia (<italic>&#x003c1;</italic>&#x0003d;0.85; <italic>P</italic>&#x0003c;0.0001).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="jpeg" xlink:href="94-6603066f3"/></fig><fig id="fig4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>MCM immunocytochemistry in oral smears. Squamous epithelial cells from normal mucosa (<bold>A</bold>) are negative when stained with antibodies against MCM 2&#x0002f;5. In sharp contrast, a cluster of cells from an oral SCC (<bold>B</bold>) are strongly positive for MCM 2&#x0002f;5 and can be identified readily, even at low magnification.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="jpeg" xlink:href="94-6603066f4"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>